Collins wrote "Another Day in Paradise" under the [[working title]] "Homeless". Collins cited an encounter with a homeless individual with two kids asking for financial assistance and the witnessing of people sleeping on an outdoor grill after the conclusion of one of Collins' concerts. He found the contrast of people lacking adequate shelter within viewing distance of [[Capitol Hill]] to be "an extraordinary contradiction". Collins discussed how these encounters informed the lyrical direction of "Another Day in Paradise".<ref name="#1">{{cite book|first=Fred |last= Bronson |year= 2003 |title= Billboard Book of Number One Hits |edition= 5th |publisher= Billboard Publications, Inc. |location= New York, NY |page=749 |isbn=0-8230-7677-6 |url-access= registration |url=https://archive.org/details/billboardbookofn0000bron_f8a3/page/748/mode/2up}}</ref>

{{blockquote|What (the song) also deals with is people's awkwardness with it. When it happened to me, I just walked straight past. I thought, I'm doing the same thing as everybody else. I felt awkward...That's what the song deals with, people just sort of starting to pretend it's not happening.<ref name="#1"/>}}

[[David Crosby]], who had met Collins at the [[Atlantic Records 40th Anniversary]] concert in [[Madison Square Garden]], provided some vocals on "Another Day in Paradise". Collins said that "David did exactly what I expected him to do. He just picked a few notes out of the air that I would never have thought of." He had originally wanted Crosby to overdub some vocals on his first solo album, ''[[Face Value (album)|Face Value]]'', but Crosby was unable to attend those sessions.<ref name="#1"/>

When determining which song to release as the first single from his solo album ''[[...But Seriously]]'', Collins selected "Another Day in Paradise" as he felt that it was different from what he had done before.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Considine |first=J.D. |author-link=J. D. Considine |date=July 1990 |title=Front Man: Phil Collins |url=https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Musician/1990/1990/Musician-1990-07.pdf |access-date=20 August 2025 |magazine=[[Musician (magazine)|Musician]] |page=7 |via=World Radio History}}</ref> The single version is slightly different from the album version in that it uses a shorter intro.{{citation needed|date=August 2025}}

"Another Day in Paradise" entered the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] at number 43 on the week dated 4 November 1989. Seven weeks later, on 23 December 1989, it became his seventh ({{As of|2025|August|alt=and, to date, final}}) No. 1 single in the U.S as a solo artist and his eighth number one hit when accounting for "[[Invisible Touch (song)|Invisible Touch]]" as a member of [[Genesis (band)|Genesis]].<ref name=Dean/><ref name="#1"/> The song was also the final No. 1 song of the 1980s in the U.S., and remained at that position for four weeks.<ref name="#1"/> The song had already reached No. 2 in the UK in November of that year.

